2, W9, T9, B9, F1\u00d72, J1, J2, J3\u00d72\n\nNo other clear melds. Possible directions:\n- Try for All Honors? Unlikely \u2014 I have too many suited terminals.\n- Try for Terminals and Honors? Possible. Most of my tiles are terminals (W8/W9/T9/B9/F1/J1/J2/J3).\n- Seven Pairs? I have several pairs: W8\u00d72, F1\u00d72, J3\u00d72. If I keep these and draw another pair among single tiles (e.g., duplicate W9, T9, etc.), it could work. But currently far from completion.\n- Outside Hand? Yes \u2014 every tile is either terminal (1 or 9) or honor. If I can form all melds/pair using such tiles, this pattern is worth 4 points.\n\nBut current issue: I have three kongs possible? No. Concealed Kongs? Not yet.\n\nBest chance: improve toward **Outside Hand** (4 points), possibly adding **All Pungs** (6 points) if I can turn some pairs into pungs. Also, **Dragon Pung** (2 points each) \u2014 I could aim to Peng J1, J2, or J3 if someone discards.\n\nBut now it's my turn \u2014 I must discard.\n\nSo, which discard minimizes danger and maximizes flexibility?\n\nLet\u2019s analyze each option:\n\n- Play B9: Bamboo 9 \u2014 no one has shown interest. B6-B7-B8 is a meld (across), B5 pung exposed. B9 hasn\u2019t been discarded much? Not seen. Possibly safe.\n- Play T9: I\u2019ve played T9 before, and others have too (next player played twice). Likely safe.\n- Play W8: I have two. W8 unshown: 4 \u2192 all still available? Initial 4 copies. Unshown: 4 \u2192 so none discarded? But wait: unshown list says W8: 4 \u2192 yes, full set unseen. So W8 is completely safe? No \u2014 I have two, so two in my hand. Unshown: 4 \u2192 total 6? Impossible! Each tile has only 4 copies.\n\nAh! Critical error check:\n\nEach tile has exactly 4 copies.\n\nMy hand: W8\u00d72 \u2192 so 2 used.\n\nUnshown: W8: 4 \u2192 cannot be. Must be max 2 remaining.\n\nTherefore, \"Unshown tiles\" list likely shows **remaining wall tiles**, not total unseen. Important!\n\nSo \u201cUnshown tiles\u201d = tiles still in wall or not revealed.\n\nThus, if unshown W8: 4 \u2192 impossible. Max possible is 2 (since I have 2).\n\nBut the list says W8: 4 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nWait \u2014 maybe it's a typo or misrepresentation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \u201cunshown\u201d means \u201cnot yet revealed to the table\u201d, i.e., including hand and wall.\n\nBut standard interpretation: \u201cunshown\u201d = remaining in wall + opponents\u2019 hands (i.e., not discarded or exposed).\n\nSo total unseen = unshown count.\n\nFor W8: unshown: 4 \u2192 but there are only 4 copies total. I have 2 \u2192 so only 2 can be unseen. So 4 is impossible.\n\nUnless the number indicates quantity still available in the game (in wall and hands), then yes: unshown = 4 - already revealed.\n\nAlready revealed: any W8 in discards or melds?\n\nFrom histories:\n- I haven\u2019t played W8\n- Next player: no W8\n- Across: no W8\n- Previous: no W8\n\nMelds:\n- Mine: W1-W2-W3\n- Next: F2,F2,F2; B3,B3,B3; W4,W5,W6\n- Across: B6,B7,B8; B5,B5,B5\n- Previous: W1,W2,W3; F4,F4,F4\n\nNo W8 in any meld. And no one has played W8 in history.\n\nSo W8: all 4 copies still in play.\n\nI have 2 \u2192 so 2 more out there.\n\nUnshown: 2 (in wall and/or opponents' hands)\n\nBut the list says W8: 4 \u2192 clearly wrong.\n\nWait \u2014 look back: \"Unshown tiles are: [...] W8: 4\"\n\nThis must mean **the number of copies still unshown (not discarded or exposed)**. Since no W8 has been discarded or exposed, and I have 2, then 2 are elsewhere \u2014 so unshown should be 2, not 4.\n\nBut it says 4. Contradiction.\n\nUnless... the number is a mistake, or it includes my hand?\n\nNo, \"unshown\" typically excludes your own hand.\n\nPossibility: the number represents **how many copies remain undiscarded/exposed**, i.e., total unseen.\n\nThen W8: 4 \u2192 means no W8 has been played or exposed \u2192 correct, because none in discard histories or melds.\n\nYes! So \u201cunshown\u201d = total number of tiles of that type still in circulation (hands + wall), not yet revealed.\n\nSince there are 4 copies, and none have been discarded or exposed, unshown = 4.\n\nI have 2 \u2192 so 2 are distributed among others or wall.\n\nSimilarly, W7: unshown: 0 \u2192 all four copies already discarded or exposed.\n\nIndeed, checking:\n- I played W7? No\n- Next player: played W7 twice? In history: \"Play W7\", \"Play W7\" \u2014 yes, two times\n- Across: played W7 once\n- Previous: played W7 once\n\u2192 total 4 plays of W7 \u2192 all discarded \u2192 so W7: 0 unshown \u2192 correct.\n\nSame for F2: unshown: 0 \u2192 and next player has Peng F2 (so 3 exposed) and previous played F2 \u2192 at least 4 \u2192 gone.\n\nF4: Peng by previous \u2192 3 exposed, and he played one earlier? History: Play F4 \u2192 yes \u2192 4 total \u2192 gone.\n\nB3: Peng by next \u2192 3 exposed, and I played B3, across played B3? My history: Play B3; across: Play B3? Across history: Play B3? No \u2014 but next: Peng B3 \u2192 3 exposed, and I played one \u2192 total 4 \u2192 so B3: 0 unshown \u2192 correct.\n\nSo unshown counts are accurate: they represent how many copies of each tile remain hidden (in hands or wall).\n\nBack to decision.\n\nI want to avoid discarding tiles that could complete an opponent\u2019s winning hand.\n\nDangerous tiles: those completing common patterns.\n\nSafe tiles: those already heavily discarded, especially terminals and honors that are fully exposed.\n\nBest candidates to discard:\n\n- F1: I have a pair. F1 is East Wind \u2014 prevalent and seat wind. If I can get a third, I get 2+2=4 points. Opponents may also want it. But F1 unshown: 4 \u2192 all still in play \u2192 so no one has discarded it. Very live. Risky to discard.\n\n- J1: unshown: 4 \u2192 all live. I have one. Dragon. Could be useful.\n\n- J2: unshown: 2 \u2192 I have one \u2192 so 1 already discarded or exposed? Total 4. I have 1, unshown: 2 \u2192 so 1 already out. Not too dangerous.\n\n- J3: unshown: 4 \u2192 all live. I have two \u2192 strong potential for Dragon Pung.\n\n- W8: unshown: 4 \u2192 all live. I have two. High character. But W4-W5-W6 is exposed (next player), W1-W2-W3 exposed (me and previous), so middle numbers are gone. W7: all discarded \u2192 so W8 might be safer, as 789 chow needs W7. With W7 gone, hard to complete 789. So W8 possibly safe.\n\n- W9: unshown: 3 \u2192 I have one \u2192 so one already discarded? Or in wall/hands. W7 gone, so 789 hard to make. Safe.\n\n- T9: unshown: 1 \u2192 I have one \u2192 total 4. So 2 already discarded or exposed? Check history:\n  - I played T9\n  - Next player played T9 twice\n  \u2192 total 3 T9 played\n  \u2192 unshown: 1 \u2192 yes, matches.\n  So only one T9 left in wall/hands. Discarding my last T9 \u2192 very safe, since three already discarded \u2014 unlikely anyone is waiting on it unless they have two already.\n\n- B9: unshown: 4 \u2192 all live. I have one. B6-B7-B8 exposed (across), B5 pung (across), so high Bamboos possible. B9 could be part of 789 chow. But B7 and B8 are already used (in B6-B7-B8), so only one copy each left? Unshown B7: 2 \u2192 B7: 4 total. Across has one in meld, so 3 left. But meld uses one, so 3 others. Unshown: 2 \u2192 so one already discarded? Across played B7? No. I didn't. Next: no. Previous: no. But across history: Play B7? No. Only Chi B7 \u2014 that was forming B6-B7-B8, so used one B7. So only one B7 exposed. Unshown: 2 \u2192 so two in circulation. B7 not exhausted.\n\nBut B9: unshown: 4 \u2192 all still available. So could be dangerous.\n\nNow, comparing safety:\n\n- T9: 3 already discarded \u2192 extremely safe\n- W7: gone, so W8 and W9 less useful for 789 chows\n- F2, F4: gone, so F1 is now the only wind left \u2014 dangerous to discard\n\nThus, safest discards: T9 or B9? T9 is better because three already out.\n\nBut I have only one T9 \u2014 discarding it loses a terminal possibility.\n\nStill, I need to choose.\n\nAnother angle: I have three pairs: F1\u00d72, J3\u00d72, W8\u00d72. If I can turn one into a kong, good.\n\nBut now, best discard is **T9**, because:\n- It has been discarded three times already \u2192 very low risk\n- I don\u2019t have a use for it in any pair or meld\n- Keeping it blocks no strategic path\n- Other tiles like F1, J3, W8 have higher potential value\n\nJ2: only one in hand, not a pair, so discard candidate.\n\nBut J2 is a dragon \u2014 could be part of Two Dragon Pungs (6 points) if I get more.\n\nBut I have only one.\n\nSimilarly, J1: one copy.\n\nBut I have J3\u00d72 \u2014 stronger incentive to keep dragons.\n\nF1\u00d72: strong.\n\nW8\u00d72: possible pair.\n\nSo the weakest links are T9, B9, J1, J2.\n\nAmong them, T9 is safest to discard due to high exposure.\n\nAlso, consider: Mixed Straight? Needs 123, 456, 789 in different suits. But W7 gone, so hard to make 789 in Characters. T7: unshown: 1 \u2192 still one left. T8: unshown: 2. T9: only one left. So 789 in Tiles possible? Barely.\n\nBut not likely.\n\nConclusion: **Play T9** is the safest and most logical.\n\nAnswer: Play T9\n\nFinal check: valid action? Yes \u2014 \"Play T9\" is in the list.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding T9 is highly safe because three copies have already been discarded (by me and the next player), minimizing the risk of feeding an opponent's win. Other options like F1, J3, or W8 have higher strategic value due to existing pairs that could develop into scoring melds (e.g., Dragon Pung, Seat Wind, Prevalent Wind). B9 remains riskier as it is completely unexposed. While J1 and J2 are singleton dragons, discarding a terminal like T9 with high exposure is preferable.\n\nReason: T9 is the safest discard due to high prior exposure (three already discarded), reducing the likelihood of it completing an opponent's hand, while preserving potentially valuable pairs in my hand.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
