re relatively safe.\n\nLooking at our hand: [W8:2, W9:1, B1:1, B4:1, F1:2, J1:1, J3:3] + Meld: [W1,W2,W3]  \nWe already have an exposed pung of Winds (W1-W2-W3 \u2014 actually this seems to be a Chow, not a Pung). Wait \u2014 correction: [W1, W2, W3] is a **Chow**, not a Pung. So no wind pung yet.  \nBut we have **F1 x2** (East Wind), which is both Seat and Prevalent Wind \u2014 very valuable.  \nAlso, **J3 x3** \u2014 that\u2019s a concealed pung of White Dragon! This gives us:\n- **Dragon Pung (2 points)** \u2013 from J3\n- **Prevalent Wind (2 pts)** and **Seat Wind (2 pts)** possible if we form a pung of F1\nCurrently F1 is only a pair. If we keep it, we may draw or claim a third later.\n\nOur best pattern prospects:\n- Concealed Pung of J3 (already qualifies for 2 points)\n- Possible All Pungs (6 points) if we convert more sets into pungs\n- Two Concealed Pungs (2 points) if we make another concealed pung\n- Mixed Double Pung or better\n\nBut currently, we have only one concealed pung (J3), one pair (F1), and many singles.\n\nHolding onto **J3** is critical \u2014 discard would risk giving away a key tile for a high-scoring hand.\n\n**Best move is to discard a less critical tile**. Among candidates:\n- B1: terminal, slight risk\n- B4: safer, middle bamboo\n- W8/W9: relatively safe due to lack of surrounding tiles\nBut note: we have **two W8s** \u2014 possibly aiming for a pair? But no immediate use.\n\nHowever, observe: **Unshown W8: 4** \u2014 meaning all 4 copies are still unshown. That implies no one has discarded any W8, so perhaps highly valued. Could be someone waiting on W8?\n\nWait \u2014 total tiles per type: 4 copies each. Unshown means not seen in discards or melds.  \nIf unshown W8 is 4, and we hold 2, then 2 remain in wall or others\u2019 hands. Not extremely rare.\n\nBut given that W7=0, W6=1, W5=1, W4=0 \u2014 the character suit 4-7 are nearly gone. So W8 cannot form 6-7-8 or 7-8-9 chows easily. Thus, W8 is relatively safe.\n\nSimilarly, W9: only 